window.SITE_INDEX = [{"href": "about-covid-19.html", "title": "About COVID-19", "desc": "Understand the disease, how it spreads, and what information was provided to the public.", "category": "Health information", "text": " What is COVID-19? COVID-19 is an infectious disease caused by the SARS-CoV-2 virus. During the pandemic, this website provided official information about symptoms, transmission, testing, vaccination, and government public health measures. Most people with COVID-19 experience a mild or moderate illness, but some become seriously unwell. Older adults and people with certain underlying health conditions may be at greater risk. How the virus spreads The virus spreads mainly through infectious respiratory particles released when a person breathes, speaks, coughs or sneezes. The risk of transmission is higher in crowded, poorly ventilated indoor spaces. Keep indoor spaces well ventilated where practical. Stay home when you feel unwell. Consider a well-fitting mask in higher-risk settings. Practise good hand and respiratory hygiene. What changed after the pandemic response? Temporary emergency rules, mandatory isolation requirements and public health orders were phased out. COVID-19 is now managed through routine public health services rather than a dedicated emergency response website. Symptoms and illness ↗ Archived symptom guidance Protecting yourself and others ↗ Everyday prevention measures End of the emergency response ↗ The final government notice "}, {"href": "symptoms.html", "title": "Symptoms of COVID-19", "desc": "Common symptoms, warning signs and archived advice on what to do if you became unwell.", "category": "Health information", "text": " Historical information — not current advice This page records information published during the Square Country COVID-19 response. Restrictions, eligibility rules, telephone services and instructions below are no longer in force. For current advice, contact the Bureau of Biosecurity and Disease Prevention. Common symptoms COVID-19 symptoms can vary. Common symptoms reported during the public health response included: Fever or chills Cough, sore throat or congestion Fatigue, headache or muscle aches Shortness of breath New loss of taste or smell Nausea or diarrhoea Symptoms alone cannot reliably distinguish COVID-19 from other respiratory infections. Seek urgent medical help Severe difficulty breathing, persistent chest pain, new confusion, or difficulty staying awake can indicate a medical emergency. Seek emergency assistance through your local medical services. Previously published advice During the emergency period, anyone with symptoms was asked to stay home, arrange testing when appropriate, and follow the isolation rules applying at that time. These rules are preserved in the historical archive. Testing for COVID-19 ↗ Historic testing arrangements Isolation and close contacts ↗ Former legal and public health requirements "}, {"href": "protect-yourself.html", "title": "Protecting yourself and others", "desc": "Everyday steps originally recommended to reduce the spread of COVID-19.", "category": "Health information", "text": " Steps to reduce infection risk The public health campaign encouraged several practical precautions. The following principles remain useful for reducing respiratory infection risks generally, though the historical policies on this site are not current. Stay home when sick: avoid close contact with others while infectious. Fresh air: open windows or improve indoor air circulation when feasible. Hand hygiene: wash hands regularly with soap and water. Respiratory etiquette: cover coughs and sneezes. Masks: consider a well-fitting mask in crowded places or around vulnerable people. Vaccination: seek individualised, up-to-date vaccine information from health professionals. People at higher risk Some people are more likely to experience severe disease. They may benefit from additional protections and early discussion with a healthcare professional about treatment options. Archived campaign materials Posters and campaign resources ↗ See the original government information materials Vaccination programme ↗ Historic rollout and eligibility "}, {"href": "testing.html", "title": "Testing for COVID-19", "desc": "PCR testing, rapid antigen tests, testing sites, and results during the response.", "category": "Health information", "text": " Historical information — not current advice This page records information published during the Square Country COVID-19 response. Restrictions, eligibility rules, telephone services and instructions below are no longer in force. For current advice, contact the Bureau of Biosecurity and Disease Prevention. Testing during the emergency response At different stages of the response, Square Country used laboratory PCR tests and rapid antigen tests (RATs). Testing arrangements changed as capacity and the public health situation evolved. Test type Historical use Result time PCR Confirmation and clinical or surveillance testing Laboratory dependent Rapid antigen test At-home screening and point-of-care testing Usually within minutes Finding a testing site Community collection centres, mobile testing units and designated health facilities were used during the pandemic. The published locations and booking procedures have been withdrawn and should not be used to plan a visit today. Positive results During the emergency phase, people who tested positive were asked to follow the isolation period then in effect and notify relevant close contacts where instructed. Those legal requirements have since expired. Isolation rules ↗ View the historical policy Response timeline ↗ See when requirements changed "}, {"href": "isolation.html", "title": "Isolation and close contacts", "desc": "A historical record of self-isolation requirements, close-contact rules and support.", "category": "Health information", "text": " Historical information — not current advice This page records information published during the Square Country COVID-19 response. Restrictions, eligibility rules, telephone services and instructions below are no longer in force. For current advice, contact the Bureau of Biosecurity and Disease Prevention. Former isolation requirements Mandatory isolation was part of the government response at various times. Requirements changed repeatedly in response to new evidence, variants and healthcare capacity. This page preserves the broad framework rather than presenting a single rule as applicable to every period. What isolation meant Stay at home or in an approved isolation setting during the required period. Limit contact with others in your household where possible. Arrange essential deliveries and support if necessary. Follow official health instructions in force at the time. Close contacts and tracing Contact tracing teams notified people who may have been exposed. At certain stages, close contacts were required to quarantine or undergo testing. Later phases relied more heavily on self-management. End of mandatory isolation Mandatory COVID-19 isolation orders are no longer active in Square Country. Personal medical advice and general infection prevention guidance have replaced the emergency restrictions. Support during isolation ↗ Former financial and social services Restrictions and public health orders ↗ Historic rules by response phase "}, {"href": "vaccination.html", "title": "COVID-19 vaccination programme", "desc": "The vaccine rollout, eligibility changes, appointments and public information programme.", "category": "Vaccination", "text": " Historical information — not current advice This page records information published during the Square Country COVID-19 response. Restrictions, eligibility rules, telephone services and instructions below are no longer in force. For current advice, contact the Bureau of Biosecurity and Disease Prevention. The national vaccination programme Square Country began its phased COVID-19 vaccination programme in 2021. Initial priority groups included frontline health workers, residents of aged care facilities and people at higher risk of severe illness. Eligibility subsequently expanded to the wider population. Historic rollout stages Phase Priority groups Purpose 1 Frontline healthcare and border workers Protect essential services 2 Older adults and medically vulnerable people Reduce severe illness and deaths 3 General adult population Broaden community protection 4 Additional eligible groups and boosters Sustain protection as guidance evolved How vaccination appointments worked Residents previously booked appointments through participating clinics and community vaccination centres. Historical booking references and clinic schedules are no longer maintained on this website. Vaccine safety and information Vaccine safety was monitored through established healthcare surveillance systems. For current recommendations, including eligibility for further doses, consult a qualified healthcare professional or the responsible health agency. Vaccination FAQs ↗ Answers from the original programme Vaccination certificates ↗ Past proof-of-vaccination requirements "}, {"href": "vaccine-faq.html", "title": "Vaccination FAQs", "desc": "Historical frequently asked questions about doses, boosters and vaccine appointments.", "category": "Vaccination", "text": " Historical information — not current advice This page records information published during the Square Country COVID-19 response. Restrictions, eligibility rules, telephone services and instructions below are no longer in force. For current advice, contact the Bureau of Biosecurity and Disease Prevention. Were COVID-19 vaccines free? Vaccines supplied through the national pandemic vaccination programme were made available without a direct charge to eligible residents. Who received the first doses? The initial priority groups included healthcare, border and essential workers, older people, and individuals at increased risk of severe disease. Did I need an appointment? Appointments were encouraged at most vaccination centres, although some pop-up clinics accepted walk-ins. Were boosters available? Additional doses became available to eligible groups as the vaccination programme evolved. Are the old vaccination centres still open? The temporary mass-vaccination sites and appointment details on this archived website are not maintained. Where can I get current recommendations? Consult a health professional or the Bureau of Biosecurity and Disease Prevention for up-to-date vaccination advice. "}, {"href": "certificates.html", "title": "Vaccination certificates", "desc": "An archive of the domestic and international vaccination certification scheme.", "category": "Vaccination", "text": " Historical information — not current advice This page records information published during the Square Country COVID-19 response. Restrictions, eligibility rules, telephone services and instructions below are no longer in force. For current advice, contact the Bureau of Biosecurity and Disease Prevention. Proof of vaccination during the pandemic During certain stages of the response, vaccination certificates were used as evidence of vaccination status for qualifying activities and travel arrangements. The requirements varied over time. Digital and paper certificates Certificates were issued through authorised systems and could be presented electronically or printed. The former public application portal has been retired. This historical website does not issue certificates. Privacy and records Personal medical records are not stored on this static archive. Requests about an individual’s immunisation history should be made through authorised health record providers. "}, {"href": "restrictions.html", "title": "Restrictions and public health orders", "desc": "Historic gathering limits, business rules, face coverings and other public health measures.", "category": "Government response", "text": " Historical information — not current advice This page records information published during the Square Country COVID-19 response. Restrictions, eligibility rules, telephone services and instructions below are no longer in force. For current advice, contact the Bureau of Biosecurity and Disease Prevention. Overview of emergency measures Square Country introduced temporary measures to slow transmission and preserve health system capacity. These included limits on gatherings, business operating conditions, face-covering requirements, isolation orders and travel controls. The exact measures depended on the response phase. Measures used during the response Area Examples of historical measures Gatherings Limits on event attendance and household mixing Businesses Capacity limits, distancing requirements and temporary closures Face coverings Requirements in certain indoor public settings Education Temporary remote learning and school infection-control measures Travel Arrival testing, entry conditions and isolation procedures Health facilities Visitor policies and infection prevention controls Are there restrictions today? No pandemic-era restrictions described on this archived site remain in force. Individual organisations may have their own infection prevention arrangements. Always check the current rules issued by relevant authorities rather than relying on archival material. Response levels ↗ Former framework and definitions Emergency legislation ↗ Orders and legal notices End of response ↗ Final government announcement "}, {"href": "response-levels.html", "title": "COVID-19 response levels", "desc": "The former alert-level framework and an explanation of each phase.", "category": "Government response", "text": " Historical information — not current advice This page records information published during the Square Country COVID-19 response. Restrictions, eligibility rules, telephone services and instructions below are no longer in force. For current advice, contact the Bureau of Biosecurity and Disease Prevention. The national response framework For part of the pandemic, public messaging organised restrictions into a four-level framework. The following is a retrospective overview of the levels and is not a legal statement of the rules at any specific time. Level Name General approach 1 Readiness Public information, hygiene and surveillance 2 Caution Additional precautions for gatherings and higher-risk settings 3 Restrictions Significant limitations on movement and gatherings 4 Emergency Strict stay-at-home measures and essential-services exemptions Movement between levels Government decisions took account of transmission patterns, hospital demand, testing capacity, and public health advice. Levels could differ by locality, and special rules sometimes applied to border or healthcare settings. Framework withdrawn The response-level system was formally discontinued as the country transitioned from emergency control to routine disease management. "}, {"href": "travel.html", "title": "Travel and border information", "desc": "Historic information for arriving in, departing from and travelling within Square Country.", "category": "Travel", "text": " Historical information — not current advice This page records information published during the Square Country COVID-19 response. Restrictions, eligibility rules, telephone services and instructions below are no longer in force. For current advice, contact the Bureau of Biosecurity and Disease Prevention. International arrivals During the pandemic, arrival requirements varied and at different times included travel declarations, pre-departure testing, quarantine, managed isolation and vaccination documentation. Domestic travel Inter-district movement was restricted during higher response levels. Essential travel exemptions and special arrangements were introduced for certain services. Border reopening Travel restrictions were progressively removed, and COVID-19 specific border procedures were eventually retired. Information about the current entry rules should be obtained from the relevant immigration and border authorities, not this archive. Border reopening timeline ↗ Historical border policy milestones Travel FAQs ↗ Previously published travel guidance "}, {"href": "border-history.html", "title": "Border reopening timeline", "desc": "The historic stages of Square Country’s international border reopening.", "category": "Travel", "text": " Historical information — not current advice This page records information published during the Square Country COVID-19 response. Restrictions, eligibility rules, telephone services and instructions below are no longer in force. For current advice, contact the Bureau of Biosecurity and Disease Prevention. Phased reopening Period Historical development 2020 Emergency border controls and arrival screening introduced 2021 Managed arrival pathways used for approved travellers Early 2022 Requirements relaxed in stages for specified travellers Late 2022 Most remaining COVID-19-specific entry measures withdrawn 2023 Border health arrangements returned to routine settings About these dates This is a fictional historical account for Square Country. It summarises the broad direction of the response and is not intended to reproduce binding legal instruments. "}, {"href": "travel-faq.html", "title": "Travel FAQs", "desc": "Common questions about border closures, quarantine and travel certificates.", "category": "Travel", "text": " Historical information — not current advice This page records information published during the Square Country COVID-19 response. Restrictions, eligibility rules, telephone services and instructions below are no longer in force. For current advice, contact the Bureau of Biosecurity and Disease Prevention. Do I need a COVID-19 test to enter Square Country now? This archive does not publish current border requirements. Check the present immigration and border guidance before travel. What was managed isolation? Managed isolation was a controlled accommodation arrangement used for some arrivals during the pandemic. Were there domestic travel restrictions? During some higher response levels, non-essential inter-district travel was restricted. Can I use a certificate from this site? No. The certificate services and links published during the emergency programme have been retired. Where are previous travel announcements? See the government announcements and border reopening archive for historical information. "}, {"href": "businesses.html", "title": "Information for businesses", "desc": "Workplace safety, financial assistance and operating requirements during the pandemic.", "category": "Support and services", "text": " Historical information — not current advice This page records information published during the Square Country COVID-19 response. Restrictions, eligibility rules, telephone services and instructions below are no longer in force. For current advice, contact the Bureau of Biosecurity and Disease Prevention. Workplace health and safety Businesses were advised to prepare continuity plans, manage employee illness, improve ventilation, and follow the public health orders applicable to their sector and response level. Operating during restrictions Keep workers and customers informed of temporary requirements. Use physical distancing and occupancy limits where ordered. Provide facilities for hygiene and cleaning. Plan for staff absences and supply chain disruptions. Economic support The government introduced temporary support programmes to assist eligible employers and sole traders affected by pandemic restrictions. These schemes have closed. Financial support schemes ↗ Historic wage and business assistance Schools and education ↗ Past education sector measures "}, {"href": "financial-support.html", "title": "Financial support schemes", "desc": "Archived information about wage support, hardship payments and economic relief.", "category": "Support and services", "text": " Historical information — not current advice This page records information published during the Square Country COVID-19 response. Restrictions, eligibility rules, telephone services and instructions below are no longer in force. For current advice, contact the Bureau of Biosecurity and Disease Prevention. Temporary pandemic support Financial measures were established to reduce the effect of restrictions on workers, households and business activity. Programmes were time limited and eligibility depended on the rules in effect during each funding round. Programme Historical purpose Current status Wage continuity payment Support eligible employers in maintaining worker incomes Closed Small business recovery grant Support eligible business operating costs Closed Isolation assistance payment Assist eligible individuals required to isolate Closed Community hardship fund Support community organisations responding to pandemic needs Closed Past applications and enquiries The online forms and claim portals associated with these schemes are no longer available. This website does not accept new applications and cannot retrieve payment records. "}, {"href": "schools.html", "title": "Schools, children and education", "desc": "School attendance, remote learning and classroom health measures during the response.", "category": "Support and services", "text": " Historical information — not current advice This page records information published during the Square Country COVID-19 response. Restrictions, eligibility rules, telephone services and instructions below are no longer in force. For current advice, contact the Bureau of Biosecurity and Disease Prevention. Education during COVID-19 Schools, childcare centres and tertiary institutions adjusted their operations according to government response levels and health guidance. At times this included remote learning, staggered attendance and temporary closures. Support for families Education providers communicated learning expectations, access to devices and wellbeing support directly with families. Arrangements differed across institutions and over time. Return to normal operation Emergency nationwide education measures were discontinued as the response ended. Current school policies are the responsibility of education and health authorities. "}, {"href": "mental-wellbeing.html", "title": "Mental health and wellbeing", "desc": "Advice and historic support information for managing stress and isolation.", "category": "Support and services", "text": " Looking after your wellbeing The pandemic affected the wellbeing of many people. Isolation, loss, uncertainty and disruption to work or study can all cause stress. This page preserves general strategies shared during the response. Maintain regular contact with family, friends or people you trust. Keep a manageable daily routine and rest. Take breaks from distressing news and social media. Stay physically active when possible. Seek support from a health professional if distress is persistent or overwhelming. Finding help The emergency counselling telephone lines and short-term funding programmes advertised in 2020–2022 are no longer maintained here. Contact current healthcare or emergency services if you need urgent assistance. "}, {"href": "support.html", "title": "Support during isolation", "desc": "Historical information about practical help, medicines, groceries and community assistance.", "category": "Support and services", "text": " Historical information — not current advice This page records information published during the Square Country COVID-19 response. Restrictions, eligibility rules, telephone services and instructions below are no longer in force. For current advice, contact the Bureau of Biosecurity and Disease Prevention. Practical assistance Isolation support services connected eligible households to food, essential medicines, welfare checks and financial assistance. Community organisations and local authorities participated in delivering support. How referrals worked Residents could request assistance through community support lines or referrals from public health staff. Those temporary response arrangements have now closed. Ongoing services People who need assistance today should use established social services, health providers or local authority channels. "}, {"href": "statistics.html", "title": "COVID-19 statistics", "desc": "Final published dashboard figures and an explanation of the historical reporting series.", "category": "Data and archive", "text": " Historical information — not current advice This page records information published during the Square Country COVID-19 response. Restrictions, eligibility rules, telephone services and instructions below are no longer in force. For current advice, contact the Bureau of Biosecurity and Disease Prevention. Historical reporting overview Throughout the pandemic, the Ministry of Health published regular case counts, testing volumes, hospital admissions and vaccination progress. Daily reporting was progressively reduced as emergency surveillance ended. Statistics archive closed The figures and charts originally hosted here were frozen after the final statistical bulletin. No live case counts are maintained on this site. 2020–23 COVID-19 emergency reporting period 2023 Final dashboard archive year Archived Status of the national daily reporting series What the dashboard measured Indicator Definition Confirmed cases Cases meeting the reporting definition in use at the time Hospital admissions Reported COVID-19-related hospitalisations Testing Tests recorded through surveillance and clinical services Vaccination coverage Share of eligible groups recorded as vaccinated Deaths Deaths attributed to or associated with COVID-19 using the applicable reporting standard Interpreting the data Definitions, testing access and reporting practices changed during the pandemic. Historical figures should not be compared across periods without considering these differences. This demonstration archive intentionally does not invent cumulative case or death totals. Reports and publications ↗ Annual and retrospective reports Response timeline ↗ Major milestones in the response "}, {"href": "reports.html", "title": "Reports and publications", "desc": "A catalogue of government statements, reviews and archived COVID-19 reports.", "category": "Data and archive", "text": " Published reports The following catalogue reproduces an illustrative set of titles for the Square Country public health archive. The summaries can be read on their respective pages. Jul 2023 Conclusion of the national COVID-19 emergency response Final transition statement and arrangements for permanent archival access. ↗ Jun 2023 COVID-19 response: lessons learned Preparedness, communications, border measures and service delivery. ↗ 2020–23 National COVID-19 surveillance summaries Historical definitions and statistical reporting background. ↗ 2020–23 Emergency orders register Overview of COVID-19 public health instruments. ↗ Using the archive Content is retained for public historical reference. Links to retired applications, forms and old advice should not be interpreted as providing a currently operating service. "}, {"href": "lessons-learned.html", "title": "Lessons learned from the response", "desc": "A retrospective summary of preparedness, public health measures and recovery.", "category": "Data and archive", "text": " Purpose of the review Following the conclusion of the emergency response, public agencies reviewed the operation of the national COVID-19 programme. This illustrative summary captures the topics examined in the Square Country archive. Areas of review Public health capacity The response highlighted the importance of surveillance systems, laboratory readiness and sufficient healthcare capacity. Communication Frequent, accessible updates helped people understand changing public health guidance. Clear records are important when advice changes over time. Community support Temporary economic and welfare measures aimed to reduce hardship caused by restrictions and isolation requirements. Preparedness for future outbreaks Lessons from the pandemic inform planning for future public health emergencies, including disease monitoring, interagency coordination and continuity of essential services. Historical context This page is part of a fictional government website created for Square Country; it is not a genuine published independent inquiry report. "}, {"href": "timeline.html", "title": "The COVID-19 response timeline", "desc": "A chronology of key events in Square Country’s pandemic response from 2020 to 2023.", "category": "Data and archive", "text": " 2020 — Emergency response Date Milestone March 2020 National coordination response established; emergency public information website launched March 2020 International border health measures introduced April 2020 Temporary gathering and movement restrictions brought into effect Mid-2020 Testing and contact tracing capacity expanded 2021 — Vaccination and changing guidance Date Milestone February 2021 Phased vaccination programme begins Mid-2021 Vaccination eligibility expands and community clinics open Late 2021 Response plans updated to account for new variants 2022 — Reopening and transition Date Milestone Early 2022 Travel and domestic restrictions start to ease Mid-2022 Testing and isolation guidance revised Late 2022 Most remaining pandemic emergency measures withdrawn 2023 — Closure of the emergency programme Date Milestone June 2023 Government releases retrospective lessons summary 21 July 2023 Final archived site update and closure of the dedicated response website About this timeline This chronology is an internally consistent, fictional history of the Square Country pandemic response. It is not a record of events in any real jurisdiction. "}, {"href": "announcements.html", "title": "Government announcements", "desc": "Official statements and historic public notices published during the national response.", "category": "Data and archive", "text": " Announcements archive The national COVID-19 response website is no longer publishing news. Previous notices are preserved for historical reference and do not constitute current government instructions. 21 Jul 2023 COVID-19 emergency response formally concluded Dedicated pandemic information services transition to archive status. ↗ 15 Jun 2023 Government publishes lessons from pandemic response Review identifies preparedness, surveillance and communications priorities. ↗ 22 Sep 2022 Remaining emergency response controls withdrawn Transition from emergency restrictions to routine respiratory disease management. ↗ 30 Jun 2022 Border reopening programme advances Additional categories of travellers can enter without pandemic-era requirements. ↗ 18 Feb 2021 National vaccination programme begins Phased programme opens to priority groups. ↗ 24 Mar 2020 COVID-19 response website launched Central information service established for the public. ↗ "}, {"href": "legislation.html", "title": "Emergency legislation and orders", "desc": "An index of the temporary powers and public health instruments used during the pandemic.", "category": "Government response", "text": " Historical information — not current advice This page records information published during the Square Country COVID-19 response. Restrictions, eligibility rules, telephone services and instructions below are no longer in force. For current advice, contact the Bureau of Biosecurity and Disease Prevention. Legal basis of the response The response involved emergency public health instruments covering isolation, travel, business operation and other temporary measures. Instruments were amended or revoked as conditions changed. Historic instrument categories Instrument category Subject Status Public health emergency declarations Emergency coordination powers Expired Movement and gatherings orders Travel and public gathering limitations Revoked Border health orders Arrivals, testing and quarantine Revoked Isolation requirements Rules for cases and contacts Revoked Workplace orders Specific operating controls Revoked Finding authoritative legal texts This page is a historical explanatory index, not a consolidated statute book. For actual Square Country legislative texts, see the relevant government legislation publication service. Visit the Square Country legislation website ↗ "}, {"href": "resources.html", "title": "Posters, resources and campaign materials", "desc": "Archived public information resources for workplaces, households and community groups.", "category": "Data and archive", "text": " Public information campaigns Downloadable posters and printed factsheets formed an important part of the government communication programme. The sample resource library below reproduces the types of materials made available to residents. Protect one another Public health awareness poster · archival reference Download TXT ↓ What to do if you have symptoms Symptoms information sheet · archival reference Download TXT ↓ Workplace safety checklist Business continuity and hygiene · archival reference Download TXT ↓ COVID-19 historical timeline Government milestones · archival reference Download TXT ↓ Accessible formats Text-based versions of the example materials are provided so they can be read by screen readers and reused offline. These are historic demonstrations and not updated medical guidance. "}, {"href": "end-of-response.html", "title": "The COVID-19 emergency response has ended", "desc": "Final government statement confirming the conclusion of the dedicated COVID-19 programme.", "category": "Data and archive", "text": " Final government announcement — 21 July 2023 The Government of Square Country has concluded the dedicated national COVID-19 emergency response. The decision follows the withdrawal of remaining emergency arrangements and the transition of COVID-19 into routine health-system management. The central public information website, covid19.gov.sc, will remain accessible as a read-only historical archive . It will no longer publish daily case statistics, operate emergency helplines, accept applications, or announce changes to COVID-19 restrictions. What this means for residents Temporary COVID-19 public health restrictions outlined on this website are no longer active. The emergency contact-tracing and isolation support services have closed. Healthcare providers continue to advise on respiratory illnesses through ordinary clinical services. Archived announcements and documents remain available for transparency and historical research. Continuing public health preparedness The Bureau of Biosecurity and Disease Prevention retains responsibility for coordinating disease surveillance and preparing for future public health threats, working with other government agencies and health providers. This website is no longer updated Last updated: 21 July 2023. This notice marks the final public update. Any present-day health advice should be obtained from current official healthcare channels. View the response timeline ↗ Read the national chronology Browse past announcements ↗ See the statement archive About this archive ↗ Learn how historic material is presented "}, {"href": "about-this-site.html", "title": "About this archived website", "desc": "Why covid19.gov.sc is kept online, what has changed and how to use its content.", "category": "About", "text": " A permanent historical record covid19.gov.sc was the central online information service for the Government of Square Country’s response to COVID-19. It published health advice, travel requirements, public health orders, service information and official announcements between 2020 and 2023. Following the end of the pandemic emergency programme, the website was preserved to support historical reference, transparency and research. Status of the content Last editorial update: 21 July 2023. Status: Read-only archive. Emergency services: Closed. Forms and applications: Not accepted through this archive. Legal guidance: Historical and not authoritative for current requirements. A note about this project Square Country and this government COVID-19 archive are fictional. The content, historic milestones and public policy descriptions are created for a simulated government website and should not be used as real medical, legal, or travel advice. Accessibility and contact Accessibility statement ↗ Inclusive access to archived information Contact and enquiries ↗ How to reach the relevant government service Site map ↗ All website pages "}, {"href": "contact.html", "title": "Contact and enquiries", "desc": "Information about contacting government services and submitting archival website feedback.", "category": "About", "text": " The COVID-19 response service has closed The pandemic call centre, email response team and online case-management services have been discontinued. This site does not provide personalised health advice, COVID-19 testing appointments or emergency assistance. General enquiries For present-day disease prevention information, please contact the Square Country Bureau of Biosecurity and Disease Prevention through its current government channels. For emergencies, use the appropriate local emergency service. Archived website feedback This demonstration form validates your input locally and displays an acknowledgement. It does not transmit or store your information. Name Email address Enquiry type Select an option Broken page or link Accessibility issue Historical content enquiry Message Preview submission Demonstration only — your message is not sent. Your form is complete. This is a static historical demonstration, so no message has been transmitted. "}, {"href": "accessibility.html", "title": "Accessibility statement", "desc": "Information about navigating this historical site using accessible technologies.", "category": "About", "text": " Our approach This archive aims to provide a clear structure, keyboard-accessible navigation, readable contrast, descriptive links and mobile-friendly layouts. Pages use semantic headings and native HTML controls. Using the site You can navigate the content using a keyboard, enlarge text using your browser, and use the search feature to locate pages. The navigation menu is operable through a button on smaller screens. Known limitations Historical material may describe services that no longer operate. This demonstration archive includes locally generated plain-text resources rather than original historical PDFs or official statistical datasets. Report a problem To report an issue with the archive interface, use the feedback form . This is a local demonstration and does not send correspondence. "}, {"href": "privacy.html", "title": "Privacy and cookies", "desc": "How this static archived website handles browser data and information you enter.", "category": "About", "text": " Information collected This static demonstration does not use a user account, tracking analytics, advertising cookies or a remote database. Searches run in your browser using a local index, and the feedback form does not transmit information. Search and forms Your search terms may appear in the address bar as a query parameter so you can share a search link. Feedback form values are not submitted to a server. External links Links to external Square Country government websites may be subject to different privacy practices and security arrangements. "}, {"href": "sitemap.html", "title": "Site map", "desc": "A complete directory of the pages available on the archived covid19.gov.sc website.", "category": "About", "text": "__SITEMAP__"}, {"href": "search.html", "title": "Search the archive", "desc": "Search historic public guidance, official statements and other COVID-19 website pages.", "category": "About", "text": "__SEARCH__"}];